<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bukhara</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bukhara is a city in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>, one of the great oasis centres of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> and of Islamic learning in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>. It was sacked by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> during the destruction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazmian-empire">Khwarazmian Empire</a> and later fell under the Chagatai and Timurid empires. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire</a> subdued it in 1740, and in the nineteenth century it became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bukhara.jpg" alt="Bukhara" width="320" height="153" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Euyasik · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kalon-Ensemble_Buchara.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1740 he campaigned in Central Asia, subduing <b>Bukhara</b> and Khiva, while keeping up military pressure on the Ottomans in the west, which ended in his victory at Kars in 1745.</blockquote><a href="/empire/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The domain included the great Silk Road cities of Samarkand and <b>Bukhara</b> as well as the broad pastoral grasslands of the Central Asian steppe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chagatai-empire">Chagatai Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Rebellions in the north around <b>Bukhara</b> led Ahmad Shah and the Uzbek Emir of Bukhara to agree that the Amu Darya would divide their lands, and he may have sought a similar sphere of influence with the Qing, as he had with the Ottomans, who divided Iran with…</blockquote><a href="/empire/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Trade linked the khanate with Russian Siberia, <b>Bukhara</b> and China: Dzungar caravans sold horses, furs and livestock in Tobolsk and other Siberian towns and, after the peace of 1739, at Qing frontier markets, while Bukharan merchants handled much of the long-distance trade.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dzungar-khanate">Dzungar Khanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ghaznavid state arose from the military household of the Samanid Empire, the Persian dynasty that ruled Khurasan and Transoxiana from <b>Bukhara</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>They may have built fortified grid-plan cities at <b>Bukhara</b>, Panjikent and Herat.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hephthalite-empire">Hephthalite Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
